  "post": {
    "caption": "A diamond shaped like half an egg. It is the Orlov, a rose cut in Russia's imperial sceptre. A rose cut has a flat back and a domed top of triangular facets that rise to a point, with no flat table. The name comes from a rosebud, and a full classic rose has 24 facets. The style began in the 1500s and stayed popular for centuries. It was reportedly made to glow softly by candlelight, and jewelers often set thin foil behind the stone to brighten it. Today rose cuts are popular again for their vintage look.",
